    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and all tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `initials` field is a derived, read-only property on the owner response. I placed each piece of logic where it belongs:\n\n1. **Domain logic \u2192 `Person`** (`model/Person.java`): added `getInitials()`, since `firstName`/`lastName` live on `Person` and this makes the rule reusable for any person subtype (Owner, Vet). A tiny private `initial()` helper keeps the method free of duplication:\n   ```java\n   public String getInitials() {\n       return initial(this.firstName) + initial(this.lastName);\n   }\n   private static String initial(String name) {\n       return (name == null || name.isEmpty()) ? \"\" : Character.toUpperCase(name.charAt(0)) + \".\";\n   }\n   ```\n   So `\"john\"` + `\"smith\"` \u2192 `\"J.S.\"`.\n\n2. **API contract \u2192 `openapi.yml`**: added an `initials` read-only string property to the `Owner` schema (alongside `displayName`), so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n3. **Mapping**: no code needed \u2014 MapStruct auto-maps `Owner.getInitials()` \u2192 `OwnerDto.initials` by property name, so `RespondWithOwner`/`OwnerMapper` were left untouched and no catch-all method absorbed the logic.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreComputesInitials` passes, as does the full suite.",
